Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so tab-separated stdin is parsed with a tab separator. Without the flag, behaviour must stay exactly as it is today.

Current state: bin/csv2json.js reads stdin and calls parse(text) without looking at any arguments. src/parse.js exports parse(text, sep = ','), which splits rows on \r?\n and fields on sep. test/csv.test.js uses node:test and already has a 'cli' test that runs bin/csv2json.js through execFileSync. package.json has "test": "node --test". README.md shows only CSV usage.

Steps:
1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether process.argv.slice(2) includes '--tsv'. The flag may appear anywhere among the arguments. If it is present, call parse(text, '\t'). Otherwise call parse(text) exactly as now. Keep the output format the same: JSON.stringify of the result followed by a single "\n". Do not change how unknown arguments are handled, do not add --help, --sep or auto-detection, and do not add any quoting rules. Fields stay split naively.
2. Leave src/parse.js alone unless you really need to change it. The existing sep parameter is enough.
3. In test/csv.test.js, or a new test/*.test.js file that node --test picks up, add tests that run bin/csv2json.js with --tsv using execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js', '--tsv'], { input, encoding: 'utf8' }) and assert the exact output:
   - input 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' -> '[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]\n'
   - input 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' -> '[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]\n'
   - input 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' -> '[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]\n'
   Also add a test that tab input without the flag still gives '[{"a\\tb":"1\\t2"}]\n' (the JSON tab escape). Keep the existing tests.
4. Update README.md. Add a section for the --tsv flag that says it reads tab-separated input, with an example invocation such as:
   node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv
   Keep the existing CSV example.
5. From the repo root, check these by hand. Each must print exactly the shown line plus a newline:
   printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo "exit=$?"   -> [{"a":"1","b":"2"}] then exit=0
   printf 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv   -> [{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]
   printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv   -> [{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]
   printf 'a,b\n1,2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js   -> [{"a":"1","b":"2"}]
   printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js   -> [{"a\tb":"1\t2"}] (with \t as the literal JSON escape)
   Then run npm test and make sure it exits 0.
6. Open one pull request with these changes. Its description should list the expect lines and say how each one is met.